Problem 1: Applying Scrum in a Telemedicine App Project
Scenario
A company developing a telemedicine mobile application is facing frequent changes in requirements. The Product Owner often modifies the Product Backlog during Sprint Planning, reducing team productivity and creating confusion among stakeholders.

1. Scrum Roles and Responsibilities
Scrum is an Agile framework that promotes collaboration, transparency, and iterative software development. It consists of three primary roles.

a) Product Owner (PO)
Responsibilities:

Defines the product vision and business goals.
Creates and prioritizes the Product Backlog.
Ensures backlog items are clear and well-defined before Sprint Planning.
Communicates stakeholder requirements to the Scrum Team.
Accepts or rejects completed work based on acceptance criteria.
Role in this scenario:
The Product Owner is frequently changing backlog items during Sprint Planning, leading to instability and reduced team focus.

b) Scrum Master
Responsibilities:

Ensures Scrum principles and practices are followed.
Facilitates Scrum events such as Sprint Planning, Daily Scrum, Sprint Review, and Sprint Retrospective.
Removes obstacles that affect team productivity.
Coaches the Product Owner and Development Team on Agile best practices.
Role in this scenario:
The Scrum Master should guide the Product Owner to finalize backlog priorities before Sprint Planning and discourage unnecessary changes during the sprint.

c) Development Team
Responsibilities:

Designs, develops, tests, and delivers the product increment.
Estimates effort for backlog items.
Maintains product quality.
Self-organizes to complete Sprint Goals.
Role in this scenario:
Frequent requirement changes interrupt development, increase rework, and reduce productivity.

2. Root Cause Analysis
Problem:
Frequent backlog modifications during Sprint Planning.

Root Cause Analysis Table
Problem	Root Cause	Impact
Frequent requirement changes	Product Owner accepts changing stakeholder requests without proper validation	Sprint goals become unstable
Poor backlog refinement	User stories are not fully prepared before Sprint Planning	Team spends excessive time discussing requirements
Lack of stakeholder alignment	Stakeholders have different priorities	Conflicting feature requests
No requirement freeze for sprint	New features are introduced during Sprint Planning	Increased rework and delayed delivery
Poor communication	Team members do not clearly understand changing priorities	Reduced productivity and confusion

