3. Improved Backlog Prioritization Plan
A structured backlog management process can reduce requirement instability.

Step 1: Regular Backlog Refinement
Conduct backlog refinement once every week.
Review upcoming user stories before Sprint Planning.
Clarify acceptance criteria.
Estimate story points in advance.
Step 2: Prioritize Using MoSCoW Technique
Priority	Description	Example
Must Have	Essential features	Video consultation
Should Have	Important but not critical	Appointment reminders
Could Have	Nice-to-have features	Dark mode
Won't Have (Current Sprint)	Deferred features	AI symptom prediction

This helps ensure critical healthcare functionality is developed first.

Step 3: Use Business Value vs. Effort Matrix
High-value, low-effort features should be implemented first.

Business Value	Development Effort	Priority
High	Low	Highest
High	High	High
Low	Low	Medium
Low	High	Lowest

Step 4: Define Sprint Goal Clearly
Example:

Sprint Goal: Implement secure patient login, doctor search, and appointment booking.

Only backlog items supporting this goal should be included in the sprint.

Step 5: Freeze Sprint Backlog
Once Sprint Planning is completed:

Avoid adding new requirements.
Emergency requests should be handled only if they are business-critical.
All other changes should be moved to the next sprint.
Step 6: Maintain Product Backlog Transparency
The Product Owner should:

Share updated priorities with stakeholders.
Explain why features are added or postponed.
Keep backlog items visible to the entire Scrum Team.
4. Retrospective Improvement Suggestions
At the end of each sprint, the Scrum Team should conduct a Sprint Retrospective to identify improvements.

Suggested Action Items
1. Improve Backlog Grooming
Hold weekly backlog refinement meetings.
Ensure user stories are complete before Sprint Planning.
2. Define Clear Acceptance Criteria
Every user story should include:

Functional requirements
Validation rules
Expected outputs
Definition of Done
3. Strengthen Stakeholder Communication
Schedule regular stakeholder review meetings.
Collect feedback before Sprint Planning rather than during the sprint.
4. Limit Mid-Sprint Changes
Follow Scrum guidelines by avoiding unnecessary backlog modifications during a sprint.
Introduce changes only after evaluating their impact.
5. Improve Estimation Accuracy
Use Planning Poker or Story Points for estimating work.
Compare estimated effort with actual effort to improve future planning.
6. Track Agile Metrics
Monitor:

Sprint Velocity
Burndown Chart
Defect Rate
Sprint Goal Success Rate
These metrics help identify planning and execution issues.

7. Continuous Team Learning
Conduct Scrum training sessions.
Share lessons learned after every sprint.
Encourage collaboration between the Product Owner, Scrum Master, and Development Team.
